I am the first who has thought it possible to write in this world with freedom; I have spoken boldly, and without reserve, of what was not to be said. I have said what is true, for fear of seeming to speak too much truth, which would have made my readers angry with me. I have given myself a licence to say things which others dared not say, because they were afraid of being censured if they said them.
There is no subject upon which I can hold my peace which will not some day bring down censure upon me from those whose censure I do not like. I believe that all good men will be pleased to see me punished for my rashness; but if I have been too rash I shall be sorry for it. My friends will perhaps think me wrong; I shall think myself right, and I shall be satisfied. I do not care whether I live or die; I know I deserve both, and I have not done enough to deserve either.

I am sure that no one will dispute that this book contains many things which ought not to be said. I have no doubt that people will say that I have not written it in the best manner. I believe that it is very badly written. I believe that I have written it in the most awkward style. I do not care whether it is written badly; I do not care whether it is badly written. If it be not written well, so much the better. I believe also that many people will say that it is not worth while to read my books; that they ought to be put into the fire as waste paper. It is not worth while to read them, and I do not care. They are not worth reading, and so much the better. I do not care how many are read; I care whether they are worth reading. I do not care whether my book is praised or blamed; I care whether it is worth praising. I want no praise; I want no blame;
